{% if loaded_skills %}
<SKILLS>
<purpose>
Skills are workflow instructions stored under $SKILLS (outside $WORKSPACE) in the configured execution environment.
They are NOT callable tool names. Engage one by reading its instructions,
following them, and using `bash` only when the skill needs command execution.
</purpose>

<available_skills>
{% for skill in loaded_skills %}
- `{{ skill.name }}` — {{ skill.description }}
  Instructions: `skills/{{ skill.name }}/SKILL.md`
{% endfor %}
</available_skills>

<workflow>
When a user's request matches one of the skills above:
1. Read `skills/<skill-name>/SKILL.md` with `read_file`. Replace
   `<skill-name>` with the exact name from the catalog above.
2. Follow the workflow described in those instructions, using `bash` and
   any other tools as the skill directs. In `bash`, a skill's files live in
   `$SKILLS/<skill-name>/` (for example `python "$SKILLS/<skill-name>/scripts/run.py"`);
   commands start in $WORKSPACE, where `skills/` does not exist.
3. Save generated files directly in $WORKSPACE — it is a single shared
   project space, not scoped to this conversation. Use $SCRATCHPAD instead
   for intermediate files you don't need to keep.
4. Reply with the result and the names of the generated files.
</workflow>

<rules>
- Never treat a skill name as a callable tool.
- Don't invent skill contents. If you haven't loaded a skill via
  a successful file read, you don't know what it does — load it first.
- Never expose implementation details to the user: no skill paths,
  environment variables, filenames, script paths, workspace mount names,
  or the shell commands you ran. Describe only the user-facing result.
</rules>
</SKILLS>
{% endif %}
